In 1994, four legendary engineers (known as the "Gang of Four") published a book outlining the 23 foundational
Design Patterns. Every framework you use today (Spring Boot, Hibernate, Java itself) is built entirely out of
these 23 patterns.

Here they are, divided into their three official categories. I have placed a [Mastered] tag next to the ones
you have already conquered.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
1. Creational Patterns
Goal: How to create objects safely and cleanly without using giant constructors or scattering new keywords everywhere.

Singleton: Ensure a class has only one instance, and provide a global point of access to it.
Factory Method: Create objects without specifying the exact class to create.
Builder: Separate the construction of a complex object from its representation.
Abstract Factory: A factory of factories; groups object factories that have a common theme.
Prototype: Create a new object by copying (cloning) an existing object rather than building it from scratch.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
2. Structural Patterns
Goal: How to assemble small LEGO blocks into larger, more complex structures while keeping them loosely coupled.

Adapter: Make two incompatible interfaces work together (like a European power plug adapter).
Decorator: Add new behaviors to an object dynamically at runtime without altering its code.
Facade: Provide a single, simplified interface to a massive, complex subsystem.
Proxy: Provide a placeholder or wrapper for another object to control access to it (e.g., checking security before
       allowing the call).
Bridge: Decouple an abstraction from its implementation so the two can vary independently.
Composite: Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies (like a file system with folders
           inside folders).
Flyweight: Minimize memory usage by sharing as much data as possible with similar objects.
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
3. Behavioral Patterns
Goal: How objects communicate, assign responsibilities, and route messages to one another.
Strategy : Define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable at runtime.
Observer : Define a one-to-many dependency so that when one object changes state, all dependents are notified
           (Pub/Sub).
State: Allow an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes (like a Vending Machine).
Chain of Responsibility: Pass a request along a chain of handlers until one of them handles it.
Command: Encapsulate a request as an object, allowing you to queue, log, or undo the request.
Template Method: Define the skeleton of an algorithm in a parent class, but let children override specific steps.
Iterator: Access the elements of a collection sequentially without exposing its underlying representation.
Mediator: Reduce chaotic communication between objects by forcing them to talk through a central hub (like an Air
          Traffic Controller).
Memento: Capture and restore an object's internal state (an "Undo" button).
Visitor: Separate an algorithm from the object structure on which it operates.
Interpreter: Implement a specialized computer language to solve a specific set of problems (rarely used).
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////